{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310757,"nonce":"FRGuOa36DEkz4Prv3NBYcCFnyQQ=","title":"FIXED e4cbf74d (LOOP UNROLLING, not the backend) — reading a `while` loop's counter AFTER the loop fails to compile (`Value IrId(N) not ...","message":"30-second repro:\n```haxe\nclass Main { public static function main() {\n    var i = 0; while (i \u003c 3) i++; Sys.println(\"i=\" + i);   // W0020, trap stub\n} }\n```\nBraced or braceless, `i++` or `i = i + 1`, in `main` or in a method, and\n`do/while` too — all fail. `for (i in 0...3)` is fine. **Drop the read after the\nloop and it compiles**, so the trigger is the EXIT phi, not the loop.\n\n**The MIR is right.** Dumped:\n```\nbb1: $1 = phi i32 [bb0: $0], [bb2: $6]      ; header\nbb3: $4 = phi i32 [bb1: $1]                  ; exit, one incoming\n```\n**IT WAS THE UNROLLER, NOT THE BACKEND.** `RAYZOR_DISABLE_PASSES=loop-unrolling`\nmade it compile and print `i=3`. Dumping the CFG the backend actually receives\n(not `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR`, which is taken BEFORE optimization) showed a STRAIGHT\nLINE with the loop gone and the header phi with it, while the exit phi still\nnamed its dest. `ir/loop_unrolling.rs` deleted the loop blocks and rewrote the\nexit phis by changing only the PREDECESSOR, keeping the value -- its own comment\nsaid \"map the value through the last iteration's register map\", but nothing did,\nand the map was dropped each iteration. Fix: keep the last iteration's map, give\nthe induction variable its EXIT value (one step past the last iteration, not the\nlast iteration's value), and refuse the unroll before touching the CFG if an\nincoming cannot be mapped.\n\nTRAP worth remembering: `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR` shows PRE-OPTIMIZATION MIR and its\n`bbN` labels are RENUMBERED, so it disagreed with the error's real block ids\n(`from_block=IrBlockId(8)`) and made the correct-looking dump misleading. To see\nwhat codegen compiles, print `blocks_to_process` inside the backend.\n\nThe backend failed in `collect_phi_args_with_coercion`\n(codegen/cranelift_backend.rs) while emitting the header block's terminator: it\nlooks up the exit phi's incoming `$1` — the header's OWN phi dest — and does not\nfind it in `value_map`. A phi dest is mapped to a Cranelift block param in\n`append_block_param(current_block, ...)`, so the suspicion is that the header's\nphi was processed while a different block was current, or the blocks are emitted\nin an order that reaches the header's terminator before its params exist. NOTE\nthe real block ids are NOT the dump's labels: the error names\n`from_block=IrBlockId(8), target_block=IrBlockId(3)`, i.e. the exit block has a\nLOWER id than the header.\n\nWorth doing: 19 of the COMPILE_FAIL rows carry `not found in value_map`, and\nreading a loop counter afterwards is ordinary code. See\n[[bugs_loop_body_conditional_update_and_exit_phi.md]] for the earlier, different\nexit-phi defect (phantom predecessor) that is already fixed.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_while_exit_phi_not_in_value_map` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}